Description: metadata: release what dead clients leave in the DB environment
 Every process that opens the metadata store allocates mutexes (9 per
 client with libdb 5.3) in the shared Berkeley DB environment in
 /dev/shm/jack_db-<uid>. A client that exits without jack_client_close()
 never returns them; once the fixed mutex region is full every client
 fails with "BDB2034 unable to allocate memory for mutex", and a jackd
 restart does not clear it, because the server joins the environment it
 finds. A client killed inside a libdb call also leaves a lock that
 blocks the server forever when it removes that client.
 .
 The environment now tracks the processes using it, the server calls
 DB_ENV->failchk at every client removal (freeing what dead processes
 held, or starting a new store when one died inside libdb), and at start
 the server runs failchk on the environment it joins and recreates it
 only when no process can use it (ENOMEM, EAGAIN, DB_RUNRECOVERY).
 .
 The three upstream commits of branch metadata-reclaim-dead-clients,
 squashed: "metadata: open the DB environment in one place",
 "metadata: release the resources of clients that died", "metadata: let
 the server start over an environment nobody can use".
Author: Pau Aliagas <linuxnow@gmail.com>
Bug: https://github.com/jackaudio/jack2/issues/988
Forwarded: https://github.com/jackaudio/jack2/pull/1030
